data.content:
Yes! Two options depending on your MSTest version:

**Option 1 — `[DataRow]`** (MSTest v2+, best fit here since your data is compile-time constants):

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DataRow("valid@email.com", true)]
[DataRow("invalid", false)]
[DataRow("", false)]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}
```

**Option 2 — Generic `TestDataRow<T1, T2>`** (MSTest v4, for when you need runtime-computed data):

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<TestDataRow<string, bool>> TestData =>
[
    new("valid@email.com", true),
    new("invalid", false),
    new("", false),
];
```

`TestDataRow<T1, T2>` (up to 16 type parameters) eliminates `object[]` casting and gives you compile-time type checking on your test data. If any row has mismatched types, the compiler catches it.

For constant data like yours, **`[DataRow]` is the simplest and recommended approach**.
